A||||9||Ronen||Painting my Fiero on my own||||5||
Z||000000||Ronen||05-11-2001||03:40 PM||||Any advise on painting my 85 Fiero GT is appreciated. Also, are there web sites with advise on how to paint my car? Its going from grey and white panels to white.||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||05-11-2001||03:52 PM||||Use the <B>search</B> At the top right of the screen!  There is a ton of info on painting a Fiero, Too many to even list. Most are all different but all have good info.<P>Use paint.... as a search word. Gool luck<BR>and I'm sure you'll get lots of replys to your question   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Here's one to start you off:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007058.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007058.html</A> <P>DXR_DAD<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||Haspers8||05-11-2001||03:54 PM||||  Check out <A HREF="http://www.autobodystore.com." TARGET=_blank>www.autobodystore.com.</A>   They have a great forum, with a nice archive.  Lots of good advice.<P>steve||||reg||14
Z||000003||FieroLisa||05-11-2001||03:58 PM||||Ronen- I just painted my car about a month ago. First of all, the search function will help you a ton, but I'll just give you my highlight of tips:<P>You don't need to take the car apart to paint it- the more panels you take off, the more chance you have of chipping when you put it all together (except the wing which you need to take off)<P>If you have a decent layer of paint on there already, all you have to do is scuff that down enough to make it completely 100% dull, no shine, and paint over that... You really only need primer if you have any major chips that you need to sand through the paint to get out. <P>Make sure you sand really well in all the crevices of the moldings, or else it will chip there if the paint doesn't stick well. It seems that lots of people who take their moldings off break the clips that hold them on, so I left everything on my car.<P>I didn't spray the paint on my car, just did all the prep and finish so can't help ya there.<P>When your done, lots of wet sanding will get out any dust that settled in your clear coat and make the paint less wavy and more shiny!  I put 3 coats of clear on mine and wetsanded a decent amount and its nice and shiny- I'm not happy till I can check my makeup in my hood when its all polished!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway hope this sorta helps!<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 violet pearl 4 speed GT: 17 in. rims, lowered, cold-air intake<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>Tarheels Fieros Website</A><P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/daytona1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by FieroLisa (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||05-11-2001||05:10 PM||||Lisa, you changed color on your car. <BR>How did you paint the doorjambs and the edges of the fenders that are under the hood and decklid when they're closed? Or did you just paint those places black? <BR>I was considering taking my panels off and painting the edges, like Ken (MooCow) did, but I'm always looking for an easier way.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-11-2001||06:05 PM||||If you're painting a color close to what's on the car, you generally don't need to take off the panels. If there's a drastic change, I would recommend it. I changed mine from black to white (about as opposite as you can get I reckon) I took everything off, and shot primer and color coat. Then I remounted everything, and am doing some additional body mods, and then I'll shoot the color coat again to balance everything out, then the poly topcoat. If you paint the mouldings, follow Lisa's advice and sand it very well, and also use a product called "Bulldog Adhesion Promotor" on them, it'll help the primer and paint to adhere alot better.<BR>Have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||05-11-2001||07:44 PM||||Mask very well with masking tape.<P>then buy about thirty cans of spray paint in the color of your choice.  <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||MrPBody||05-11-2001||09:05 PM||||I recently painted my formerly silver/gray '85 SE:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newyel01.jpg">  <P>I removed and painted the front fenders, hood, and lower quarters.  At that point, my friend's air compressor broke, so I let Maaco finish the job.  I think my work is better than Maaco's.<P>Most of the work is preparation.  And you need a clean, warm, well-ventilated place to paint.  The painting itself wasn't too tough.<P>Raydar --<P>Front hood jambs are part of front fenders; door jambs are part of lower quarters.  If you paint those off the car, the only jamb you really need to mask is the trunk.<P>. . . or maybe you already know that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroLisa||05-12-2001||12:58 AM||||Raydar- doorjambs are still black, didn't do anything to those- anything and everything that was gold, we taped off and painted that purple.  We did remove the hood the second time we painted it (Driving down to daytona beach from Ohio on a fresh coat of paint doesn't resist stone chips well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  )  But that was only cause we didn't want to tape off the whole front end again. I have since driven it about 700 miles and have had no problems whatsoever with the paint chipping or anything.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||05-13-2001||10:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B><BR>Front hood jambs are part of front fenders; door jambs are part of lower quarters.  If you paint those off the car, the only jamb you really need to mask is the trunk.<P>. . . or maybe you already know that.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah. But I always look for the hard way to do stuff. Inherited that from my dad. <P>But you're right. Probably all I'd have to remove would be the fenders, hood and lower quarters. I'd spray the edges that are normally under the other panels, as well as the holes in the hood where the headlight doors fit. Don't know WHAT other panels I thought I'd have to remove. <BR>I also want to remove my side moldings. That should be a lot of fun. <BR>I'm going to be changing from black to dark silver/pewter. I'm counting on it being easier than than covering a bright color. Like red.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Thanks, Lisa. I'll remember about the stone chips, too. We have a quarry near here, and the dumptrucks are hell! Not just the stones, but sand and grit, too.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
